  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 1255 AM MDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nAn upper level high over the Desert Southwest will continue to \nmove slowly northeastward. Northwest\u00a0flow aloft around the high \nwill persist today, continuing to bring smoke from the Pacific \nNorthwest. Temperatures will warm up more today, with highs \nreturning to the 90s. The airmass will be dry with mostly sunny \nskies prevailing throughout the\u00a0day.\u00a0\n\nFor Friday and the weekend, the upper level high slightly \nstrengthens Friday and stalls over the Four Corners on Saturday \nand Sunday. The warmest air is expected Saturday and Sunday, with \nhighs reaching 97-105 across northeast Colorado. With the ridge \ncentered over the Four Corners, we will maintain a weak west-\nnorthwest flow aloft, which is expected to continue transporting \nwildfire smoke into the region.\u00a0This could affect high \ntemperatures over the next several days. Today will offer a \nglimpse of how much this smoke will affect high temperatures. We \nare on track for Heat Advisories for Saturday and Sunday, but \ntemperatures could fall short due to the wildfire smoke. The \nairmass stays very dry (precipitable water values 50% of normal) \nFriday and Saturday. Moisture finally starts to increase Sunday, \nthough\u00a0precipitable\u00a0water values stay below normal. We should see \na few showers and storms form over the higher elevations Sunday \nafternoon.\n\nFor next week Monday through Wednesday, the upper level high \nweakens some. Models differ on the location and strength of the \nupper level high. The GFS keeps the high parked over the Four \nCorners and only weakens the ridge slightly. This would limit \nmonsoonal moisture and help keep temperatures very warm. \nMeanwhile, the ECMWF shows the ridge weakening more and ending up \nover New Mexico and Texas. This leads to a better monsoonal fetch,\ncooler temperatures, and a better chance for showers and \nthunderstorms.",
